WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.402
Natasha has so many issues on the table.

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:03.303
For those striking,

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:03.837
I understand

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.972
you are at a picket line

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:08.141
at the Netflix studios in Los Angeles.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:08.775
Just tell us what

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:11.244
union members are saying to you there.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.078
Yeah.

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:12.946
All right.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:13.646
Well, Cristina,

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:15.415
a lot of discussion around

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:17.217
better compensation,

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:19.052
the protections regarding

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.253
artificial intelligence sense

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.054
where I could take

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:22.989
the digital likeness of an actor

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.424
and residuals

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:26.693
in the world of streaming services.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:27.260
And that's where

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.029
I want to bring in our guest,

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.563
actor Britney Gamez.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:33.133
And you have been doing some shows

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:35.702
and content for Netflix, for Hulu.

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.904
Tell me about what you were saying

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:39.406
earlier, that your brother is

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:40.974
also an actor

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.275
and was just receiving

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:43.877
a residual check for something

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:44.978
he did 30 years ago.

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:46.413
Yeah, my brother was an actor.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:47.147
He's since retired,

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:48.715
but in the nineties worked a lot.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.617
And we sometimes get residuals

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:52.485
and he'll get checks from broadcast

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:54.788
television shows that he did in 1995

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.423
that are worth more than residual

00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:58.725
checks from big corporations for films

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:00.460
I did in 2020.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.294
And that is

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.195
I think a big part of the reason

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:03.296
why people are out here today.

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:03.830
I understand

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:04.431
that streaming

00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:06.866
is such an uncharted territory,

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.401
but there has to be some sort of cap

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.170
because they're making money.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.172
All these millionaires are making money,

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:12.839
but nobody else

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:14.574
is seeing any of that money.

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.275
Tell me a little bit

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:15.742
about what

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:17.310
your life looks like

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.412
when you are doing a lot of content

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.247
for these streaming services.

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.516
Does it mean that you have to do extra

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.452
work for your day job just to,

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:27.287
you know, connect those moments? Right.

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:28.254
I mean, for me

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:28.922
personally, it's

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.023
been a lot of the in-between.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.491
There's a lot of waiting and acting.

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:33.326
And I think in the days of pass,

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:34.694
you were able to get through

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:36.763
from job to job based off residuals.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.064
And that is sort of gone away.

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:39.399
I mean, SAG represents

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:40.934
160,000 members,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.203
95% of which can't pay their bills.

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.070
I mean, a lot of actors

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.305
that I have worked with

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:45.905
that I'm doing shows

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.041
would have to have side jobs in between.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:48.842
I have to do side jobs

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.443
while working just to make ends meet

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:51.211
because the money is

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:52.712
just not what it used to be.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.447
Even while they're on a show.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:55.515
Yeah, absolutely.

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:56.583
100%. Yeah.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.785
And then tell me about your concerns

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:00.053
regarding A.I..

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.921
What are your feelings on that right now?

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:02.322
I mean, it's

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:04.190
such a scary, uncharted territory.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.758
I don't know too much about it,

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:07.560
but I know that the big threat right now

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.795
is that they can sort of

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:09.796
take your likeness

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.131
and use it for whatever they want.

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:11.564
I know it's

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.233
an even bigger threat to the Writers

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:13.399
Guild,

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:15.034
which is obviously out here tonight.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.137
But so a corporation can pay

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:18.338
you a sag de ray

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:19.272
and that's supposed to last you

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:20.140
for months and months on end,

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.841
but they can use your likeness for years

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.243
and years and years.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:24.177
How are you feeling today?

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:24.577
It's day

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:25.712
one for SAG-AFTRA,

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:27.013
but it's day 70

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.715
something for the Writers' strike,

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:29.582
which tells you

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:30.750
that this could be

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.819
a very extended experience.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.320
I'm feeling really excited.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:34.921
I've been out here

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:35.688
quite a few times

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:36.723
in support of the writers,

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.458
and the energy has always been good

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:40.026
but nothing like it is today.

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.427
Everybody is excited,

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.362
everybody is loud,

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:42.929
and I hope that we

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.297
just keep that energy going.

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.267
Is there anything that you're

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:49.269
hearing from the studios

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:51.671
where you want to send a message back?

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:52.138
I mean,

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.073
I just think you can't replace us.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:54.440
What do you

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.742
what do you think you're doing?

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:56.242
My guy

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:57.310
like you think

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.344
that artificial intelligence

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.812
is going to be able to create stories

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.614
and tell the stories of our lives.

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.183
You can't do that.

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.317
Writers can't be replaced.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.019
Actors can't be replaced.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:06.719
Thank you, Britney.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:07.620
And we're already

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.757
seeing a lot of financial impact

00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:12.492
from the Writers' strike

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:13.459
that has been going on

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.594
for more than 70 days.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.696
And now with SAG, after joining them

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:18.932
we've talked to a number

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.767
of local businesses,

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:23.269
people who run restaurants, janitors,

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.905
dry cleaners, makeup artists

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.741
who are not seeing their business

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:29.175
come in

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.311
because productions are at a standstill.

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:33.012
I did speak to the Milken

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.515
Institute's global chief strategist

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:36.349
who said that

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.684
that is going to be seen

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:40.820
now much more abroad as well outside

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.256
the United States in filming hubs

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:44.490
like the U.K.,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.392
like Australia, like New Zealand,

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.827
and the global impact

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:50.763
could be at least $4 billion with the B

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:52.365
casino. Wow.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:52.966
Wow.

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:54.100
Well, as you say, this is,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.301
of course, a global issue,

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.536
not just in the United States.

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:57.737
And it seems, from what

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:58.671
your guest was saying there,

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:00.506
Natasha, that they are prepared to strike

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:02.575
for as long as it takes.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.711
Natasha Chen there live from L.A..

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.512
Thanks for bringing us that Natasha.

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:07.614
And I'm glad that Natasha's

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:08.848
guest, Brittany, mentioned.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:09.816
We want to talk about that

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:11.918
because the strike comes after actor

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:13.620
slammed the proposal from the studios

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.588
on how artificial intelligence

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:16.522
would be used in

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:17.924
movies and television shows.

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:20.593
In that groundbreaking A.I.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:21.127
proposal,

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.262
they propose

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.796
that our background performers

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.365
should be able to be scanned,

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.166
get paid for one day's pay,

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:27.934
and their company

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:28.668
should own

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:29.469
that, scan

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:31.104
their image, their likeness,

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:32.505
and to be able to use it

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.474
for the rest of eternity in any project

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:35.108
they want

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.310
with no consent and no compensation

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.247
Well, Nina Schick is joining me now.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:41.814
She's an author

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:43.216
and commentator specializing

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:44.417
in generative A.I.

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:44.717
And in a

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:45.551
thank you for

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.787
joining us on this important day.

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:48.721
We had a taste

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:49.188
just then

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:52.091
in that short clip we showed us of how

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:54.394
what impact, you know, the use of A.I.

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:54.994
could have.

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:56.663
And it's quite frightening, frankly,

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:57.964
to see it laid out.

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.265
You have called

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:00.700
Fran Drescher is speech,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.068
that powerful speech

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:03.102
that's kind of been

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:04.804
at the front of every news line on this,

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:07.607
a moment of historical significance.

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:09.275
Can you just begin

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:11.511
by explaining how justified she is

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:12.378
and everyone else

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:13.479
in the industry

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.547
by their fear

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:16.616
that I could take over their jobs?

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:18.751
I think that this is going to become

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:21.054
one of the most personal debates

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:23.089
in society of long been

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:24.324
predicting that this is going

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:26.292
to get intensely political

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:27.360
because for the first time,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.462
if you kind of consider the technology

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:31.197
revolutions of the past 30 years,

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:32.598
from the Internet to the smartphone,

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:33.566
it's all been about

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.601
building a digital ecosystem

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.370
and digital infrastructure

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.971
However, when it comes to A.I.

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.973
and in particular generative A.I.,

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.709
you can conceive of it

00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:45.712
as a knowledge and creative revolution.

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:47.980
For the first time, you have machines

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.649
that are able to do

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.384
creative, intelligent output,

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:51.918
which we thought

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:53.853
was only unique to humans, right?

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:56.255
So the capabilities of Egypt

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:57.690
or the capabilities

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.025
of artificial intelligence

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.427
to actually clone people's biometrics.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:02.562
So it's not

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:04.630
only the entertainment industry

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:05.531
that's going to be

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.266
utterly disrupted by this.

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:08.434
But I would argue that

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:10.103
all forms of knowledge

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:12.305
work, white collar work, creative works,

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.107
are going to be impacted

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:16.175
by the effects of artificial intelligence

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.277
and the key philosophical question.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.079
This is the thing that Fran is getting at

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:21.781
and that SAG is getting at

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:22.382
is to

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:25.685
what extent is this going to augment

00:06:25.685 --> 00:06:26.853
or automate us?

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:27.320
And obviously,

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.055
the fear with this action

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.290
is that it is going to automate us

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:32.358
and that we're not going

00:06:32.358 --> 00:06:33.559
to be fairly compensated.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.028
And what we're seeing with this revolt

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:37.530
really is the sort of beginnings

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.931
of this being voiced

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:40.566
really publicly for the first time

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:41.367
and for us

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:42.969
beginning to really understand

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:44.137
the ramifications of this,

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:45.071
as you laid out.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.173
It seems to me that the question

00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:49.175
of how far this goes, you know,

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:50.710
in this industry,

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:51.778
in this particular industry

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:53.846
depends on where the power lies.

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:54.914
Right at the moment,

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:57.016
we're seeing big names, big

00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:57.617
acting names

00:06:57.617 --> 00:06:59.786
come out in support of lesser actors.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:02.155
But if that support wanes over time,

00:07:02.155 --> 00:07:02.622
the question

00:07:02.622 --> 00:07:03.089
is, you know,

00:07:03.089 --> 00:07:05.958
how much leverage are industry insiders

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:09.662
going to have to prevent the use of AI

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:11.864
actually being used in this industry?

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:13.399
So I don't think that we are going

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:14.434
to prevent the use

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:15.735
of artificial intelligence

00:07:15.735 --> 00:07:16.869
either in entertainment

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.304
or any other industry.

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:19.605
I think it's going to actually become

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.040
a fundamental part

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.175
of knowledge work

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.076
and creative work going forward.

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:25.445
But the question

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:28.414
as to how we put in place protections

00:07:28.414 --> 00:07:29.315
and perhaps

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:30.883
in this case more,

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:32.051
how do we share

00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:33.486
some of the incredible

00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:35.822
abundance and economic abundance

00:07:35.822 --> 00:07:36.989
that's going to come with this

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:38.257
AI powered revolution

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:39.392
and ensure

00:07:39.392 --> 00:07:41.260
that, for instance, in this case,

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.161
if actors

00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:43.863
digital likeness is being used,

00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:45.364
that they're being fairly compensated

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:45.698
for that

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:46.866
because that should be a part

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:48.100
of their IP. Right.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.536
But what Duncan from SAG-AFTRA

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:51.571
was referencing

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:52.505
was that

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:54.507
the studios were saying in perpetuity,

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:56.609
we now own the rights to your likeness.

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:57.477
This is not what we see

00:07:57.477 --> 00:07:58.878
happening with big Hollywood stars.

00:07:58.878 --> 00:07:59.712
For instance,

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:00.780
you might have seen

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:01.781
the recent

00:08:01.781 --> 00:08:03.082
ad which came out with Jennifer

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:04.750
Lopez for

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:05.885
Virgin Atlantic,

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:07.520
I think it was, where it's an air

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.654
simulated version of her.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:10.356
She's being compensated for that.

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:11.491
But our background actors

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:12.625
can be compensated, too.

00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:14.026
So if you were advising

00:08:14.026 --> 00:08:16.028
members of SAG-AFTRA, like what to do?

00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:17.230
I mean, what protections

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:18.397
would you say to them,

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:19.465
you know, go get these.

00:08:19.465 --> 00:08:20.366
This is what you need

00:08:20.366 --> 00:08:21.334
for the future protection?

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.703
We were actually at an event together

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:24.437
in January

00:08:24.437 --> 00:08:26.038
and at the time,

00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:28.140
a lot of the conversation was about how,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:28.541
you know,

00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:29.375
this was

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:30.943
something that they were looking at,

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:31.811
but they didn't think that

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:33.012
it would be such a big deal.

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:34.080
And actors and writers

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:34.947
couldn't be replaced.

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:35.982
Now, six months,

00:08:35.982 --> 00:08:37.850
just six months later,

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:40.052
the conversation is entirely different.

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:41.587
And it's very interesting

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.422
to see SAG and Fran

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:43.856
kind of

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:45.358
putting themselves

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:48.127
at the front line of what she pitched

00:08:48.127 --> 00:08:49.862
very clearly in her speech

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:52.798
as a bigger battle for labor.

00:08:52.832 --> 00:08:53.132
Right.

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:54.567
She said we are just the first,

00:08:54.567 --> 00:08:55.501
but it isn't only about

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:56.802
the entertainment industry.

00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:58.905
This is for all workers.

00:08:58.905 --> 00:09:00.940
Now, she said this is a historic moment.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:01.741
And indeed,

00:09:01.741 --> 00:09:02.508
I do think

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:04.877
that speech has historic significance

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:07.380
because the issues and the fears

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:08.447
that she raised

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:09.615
are increasingly going

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:10.816
to become a core part

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:12.285
of public discourse,

00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:14.053
discourse far beyond

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:15.988
I think, the entertainment sector.

00:09:15.988 --> 00:09:17.356
I just want to show,

00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:18.391
as we're referencing

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:19.358
sort of real life events

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:20.493
here, our viewers, a clip

00:09:20.493 --> 00:09:22.595
from the recent Marvel movie

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:25.831
Marvel TV series where the studios

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:27.099
basically used air

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:28.935
and the opening credits.

00:09:28.935 --> 00:09:30.536
They didn't use any graphic designers.

00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:32.071
They didn't use any animators.

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:32.338
I don't know

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:33.606
if we can run that clip for you.

00:09:33.606 --> 00:09:34.607
Here it is.

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:36.042
It was a kind of artistic take.

00:09:36.042 --> 00:09:37.109
And this caused outrage,

00:09:37.109 --> 00:09:37.743
of course,

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:39.512
because of how easily

00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:41.247
people could be replaced.

00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:42.381
But it also raises

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:43.082
issues

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:43.849
as we've been talking

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:46.052
about, of copyright.

00:09:46.152 --> 00:09:46.819
You know, that

00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:47.920
that speaks to your point

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.823
as well about bodies coming for actors.

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:52.525
I mean, is it

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:55.261
just the issue of copyright exist in AI?

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:55.995
Could that be used

00:09:55.995 --> 00:09:57.063
as a potential protection?

00:09:57.063 --> 00:09:59.198
It is unprecedented territory

00:09:59.198 --> 00:10:00.266
and it is a territory

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:02.335
that is being litigated right now.

00:10:02.335 --> 00:10:04.370
So some of the breakthroughs that you see

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.673
in this capability to generate to create

00:10:07.974 --> 00:10:10.910
intelligent and creative work is nascent.

00:10:11.077 --> 00:10:13.813
But already since the release of catch up

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:14.880
last November,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.849
the conversation has dramatically shifted

00:10:16.849 --> 00:10:18.684
to full capacity.

00:10:18.684 --> 00:10:21.087
Capability and scale of this technology

00:10:21.087 --> 00:10:21.954
is becoming apparent.

00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:23.289
And a part of that

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:25.257
is that we've seen a bunch of class

00:10:25.257 --> 00:10:26.225
action lawsuits

00:10:26.225 --> 00:10:28.094
launched in the United States

00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:29.462
against some of the creators

00:10:29.462 --> 00:10:31.697
of these large general models

00:10:31.697 --> 00:10:32.665
in which they argue

00:10:32.665 --> 00:10:33.866
that the training data

00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:36.135
used to create the synthetic or air

00:10:36.168 --> 00:10:37.169
made content

00:10:37.169 --> 00:10:39.271
is subject to copyright protection.

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:40.539
Now, that's all winding its way

00:10:40.539 --> 00:10:41.340
through the courts.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:42.341
It's going to be a big one.

